{"id":"fm-fig-017","s":"further","t":"fp-algebra","lv":3,"q":"The graph shows $y=f(x)$ with its two asymptotes dashed. P and Q are the turning points. Which set gives the range of $f$?","o":["$y \\le 0$ or $y \\ge 4$","$-2 < y < 6$","$y \\le -2$ or $y \\ge 6$","$y \\in \\mathbb{R}$, $y \\ne 2$"],"a":2,"x":"The left branch has its maximum at P, where $y=-2$, and falls to $-\\infty$. The right branch has its minimum at Q, where $y=6$, and rises to $+\\infty$. No curve lies between $y=-2$ and $y=6$, so $y\\le -2$ or $y\\ge 6$.","w":["Uses the $x$-coordinates of P and Q (0 and 4) instead of their $y$-coordinates.","Takes the values between the turning points as the range, but this is the gap where the curve does not exist.","","Treats the vertical asymptote $x=2$ as if it excluded a $y$-value, ignoring the gap between the turning points."],"fig":{"type":"graph","x":[-4,8],"y":[-8,10],"xstep":2,"ystep":2,"xlabel":"x","ylabel":"y","lines":[{"pts":[[-4,-4.667],[-3,-3.8],[-2,-3],[-1,-2.333],[0,-2],[0.25,-2.036],[0.5,-2.167],[0.75,-2.45],[1,-3],[1.1,-3.344],[1.25,-4.083],[1.4,-5.267],[1.5,-6.5],[1.55,-7.339],[1.58,-7.944]],"c":0},{"pts":[[2.55,9.823],[2.6,9.267],[2.7,8.414],[2.8,7.8],[3,7],[3.25,6.45],[3.5,6.167],[3.75,6.036],[4,6],[4.5,6.1],[5,6.333],[6,7],[7,7.8],[8,8.667]],"c":1},{"pts":[[2,-8],[2,10]],"dash":true,"label":"x = 2","c":2},{"pts":[[-4,-4],[8,8]],"dash":true,"label":"y = x","c":3}],"points":[{"at":[0,-2],"label":"P"},{"at":[4,6],"label":"Q"}],"xnums":true,"ynums":true}}
{"id":"fm-fig-018","s":"further","t":"fp-de","lv":2,"q":"The graph shows displacement against time for three systems released from the same position at rest, each with different damping. Which option correctly identifies curves A, B and C?","o":["A overdamped, B underdamped, C critically damped","A underdamped, B critically damped, C overdamped","A critically damped, B underdamped, C overdamped","A critically damped, B overdamped, C underdamped"],"a":2,"x":"Curve B crosses $x=0$ and oscillates, so it is underdamped. A and C never cross the axis. A returns to equilibrium faster than C without oscillating, so A is critically damped and C is overdamped.","w":["Treats the slowest-decaying curve as critical damping. Critical damping is the fastest non-oscillating return, so it lies below the overdamped curve.","Calls the curve with the fastest non-oscillating fall underdamped and the oscillating curve critical. Only underdamped motion crosses the equilibrium line.","","Sees B as overdamped and C as underdamped, ignoring that only B crosses $x=0$. Only underdamped motion oscillates about equilibrium."],"fig":{"type":"graph","x":[0,2.5],"y":[-0.4,1.2],"xstep":0.5,"ystep":0.2,"xlabel":"time / s","ylabel":"displacement / m","lines":[{"pts":[[0.0,1.0],[0.015625,0.999522],[0.03125,0.998126],[0.046875,0.995871],[0.0625,0.992809],[0.078125,0.988993],[0.09375,0.984472],[0.109375,0.979293],[0.125,0.973501],[0.140625,0.967138],[0.15625,0.960246],[0.171875,0.952861],[0.1875,0.945023],[0.203125,0.936764],[0.21875,0.92812],[0.234375,0.91912],[0.25,0.909796],[0.265625,0.900175],[0.28125,0.890286],[0.296875,0.880152],[0.3125,0.8698],[0.328125,0.859251],[0.34375,0.848528],[0.359375,0.837652],[0.375,0.826641],[0.390625,0.815516],[0.40625,0.804292],[0.421875,0.792987],[0.4375,0.781616],[0.453125,0.770195],[0.46875,0.758736],[0.484375,0.747253],[0.5,0.735759],[0.515625,0.724264],[0.53125,0.712781],[0.546875,0.701318],[0.5625,0.689886],[0.578125,0.678494],[0.59375,0.66715],[0.609375,0.655861