{"id":"fm-fig-016","s":"further","t":"fm-mom","lv":2,"q":"The graph shows the force on a ball from a wall during an impact. Use it to find the magnitude of the impulse the wall exerts on the ball.","o":["3.2 N s","300 N s","2.4 N s","2400 N s"],"a":2,"x":"Impulse is the area under the force-time graph. Trapezium: parallel sides 8 ms and 4 ms, height 400 N, so area = ½(8+4)×400 = 2400 N ms. Convert: 2400 × 10⁻³ = 2.4 N s.","w":["Multiplied the peak force by the whole duration, treating the pulse as a rectangle instead of finding the area under the graph.","Gave the mean force, area ÷ duration, instead of the impulse, which is the area itself.","","Found the area correctly as 2400 N ms but forgot to convert milliseconds to seconds."],"fig":{"type":"graph","x":[0,8],"y":[0,500],"xstep":1,"ystep":100,"xlabel":"time / ms","ylabel":"force / N","lines":[{"pts":[[0,0],[2,400],[6,400],[8,0]],"c":0}]}}
